{"description":"Detailed whiteboard explanation of database sharding: splitting data across shards, routing, shard keys, scaling, replication, rebalancing, and trade-offs.","commands":[{"type":"text","x":470,"y":55,"text":"DATABASE SHARDING","fontSize":30,"color":"#1e293b","explain":"Let's start with the big idea: sharding splits one large database across multiple smaller databases."},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"rect","x":90,"y":130,"width":250,"height":150,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#e2e8f0","explain":"First, imagine one database holding every customer record; this becomes difficult to scale as data grows."},{"type":"text","x":155,"y":165,"text":"ONE DATABASE","fontSize":22,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":145,"y":205,"text":"Users: 1–12M","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":130,"y":240,"text":"Huge table","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"arrow","x1":345,"y1":205,"x2":455,"y2":205,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#2563eb","explain":"Now we introduce sharding, sending different pieces of the data to different database servers."},{"type":"text","x":360,"y":175,"text":"SHARD","fontSize":18,"color":"#2563eb"},{"type":"rect","x":470,"y":130,"width":190,"height":115,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"Shard 1 stores one portion of the records, so its workload is smaller."},{"type":"text","x":520,"y":155,"text":"SHARD 1","fontSize":22,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":515,"y":195,"text":"Users 1–4M","fontSize":19,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":690,"y":130,"width":190,"height":115,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dcfce7","explain":"Shard 2 independently stores another portion of the data."},{"type":"text","x":740,"y":155,"text":"SHARD 2","fontSize":22,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":735,"y":195,"text":"Users 4–8M","fontSize":19,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":910,"y":130,"width":190,"height":115,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#fef3c7","explain":"Shard 3 stores the remaining records, allowing the system to scale horizontally."},{"type":"text","x":960,"y":155,"text":"SHARD 3","fontSize":22,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":955,"y":195,"text":"Users 8–12M","fontSize":19,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"text","x":75,"y":335,"text":"HOW A REQUEST IS ROUTED","fontSize":26,"color":"#1e293b","explain":"The key question is: how does the application know which shard contains a requested user?"},{"type":"ellipse","cx":170,"cy":415,"rx":65,"ry":42,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#f1f5f9","explain":"A client sends a normal request, such as asking for one user's profile."},{"type":"text","x":135,"y":410,"text":"CLIENT","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"arrow","x1":235,"y1":415,"x2":355,"y2":415,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#2563eb","explain":"The request reaches a router or application layer that knows the sharding rule."},{"type":"rect","x":355,"y":370,"width":210,"height":90,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"The router calculates the shard from the shard key, such as user_id."},{"type":"text","x":405,"y":392,"text":"SHARD ROUTER","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":410,"y":425,"text":"user_id → shard","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"arrow","x1":565,"y1":390,"x2":700,"y2":345,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#2563eb","explain":"If the key maps to shard 1, the router sends the query there."},{"type":"arrow","x1":565,"y1":415,"x2":700,"y2":415,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#16a34a","explain":"Another key can map directly to shard 2 without querying every database."},{"type":"arrow","x1":565,"y1":440,"x2":700,"y2":485,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#d97706","explain":"A different key can instead be routed to shard 3."},{"type":"rect","x":700,"y":300,"width":180,"height":80,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"This database receives only the requests belonging to its partition."},{"type":"text","x":750,"y":330,"text":"SHARD 1","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":700,"y":375,"width":180,"height":80,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dcfce7","explain":"Shard 2 handles its own subset, keeping traffic distributed."},{"type":"text","x":750,"y":405,"text":"SHARD 2","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":700,"y":450,"width":180,"height":80,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#fef3c7","explain":"Shard 3 handles another subset, so no single server must carry everything."},{"type":"text","x":750,"y":480,"text":"SHARD 3","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"text","x":70,"y":585,"text":"COMMON SHARDING STRATEGIES","fontSize":26,"color":"#1e293b","explain":"There are several ways to decide which records belong on which shard."},{"type":"rect","x":80,"y":630,"width":300,"height":130,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#f1f5f9","explain":"Range sharding assigns ranges of values to different shards; it is simple but can create hot spots."},{"type":"text","x":145,"y":660,"text":"RANGE SHARDING","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":120,"y":700,"text":"1–4M | 4–8M | 8–12M","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":410,"y":630,"width":300,"height":130,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"Hash sharding hashes the shard key, usually spreading records more evenly."},{"type":"text","x":475,"y":660,"text":"HASH SHARDING","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":450,"y":700,"text":"hash(user_id) → shard","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":740,"y":630,"width":300,"height":130,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dcfce7","explain":"Directory-based sharding uses a mapping table that explicitly tells the router where each key belongs."},{"type":"text","x":790,"y":660,"text":"DIRECTORY","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":770,"y":700,"text":"key → assigned shard","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"text","x":75,"y":825,"text":"CHOOSING A GOOD SHARD KEY","fontSize":26,"color":"#1e293b","explain":"The shard key is crucial because a poor choice can overload one shard or make queries expensive."},{"type":"ellipse","cx":210,"cy":900,"rx":115,"ry":55,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dcfce7","explain":"A strong shard key distributes records and traffic evenly across the cluster."},{"type":"text","x":150,"y":895,"text":"EVEN LOAD","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"ellipse","cx":510,"cy":900,"rx":115,"ry":55,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"It should also match common query patterns so requests can find one shard quickly."},{"type":"text","x":445,"y":895,"text":"QUERY LOCALITY","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"ellipse","cx":810,"cy":900,"rx":115,"ry":55,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#fef3c7","explain":"The key should avoid concentrating writes or popular records on a single shard."},{"type":"text","x":745,"y":895,"text":"AVOID HOTSPOTS","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"text","x":75,"y":1000,"text":"SHARDING + REPLICATION","fontSize":26,"color":"#1e293b","explain":"Sharding spreads data for scale, while replication creates copies for availability and failover."},{"type":"rect","x":90,"y":1050,"width":220,"height":90,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dbeafe","explain":"Each shard can have a primary database that accepts writes."},{"type":"text","x":150,"y":1080,"text":"SHARD 1","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":140,"y":1110,"text":"Primary","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"arrow","x1":310,"y1":1095,"x2":390,"y2":1095,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#2563eb","explain":"The primary can replicate its data to another server for redundancy."},{"type":"rect","x":390,"y":1050,"width":220,"height":90,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#f1f5f9","explain":"The replica can take over if the primary fails and can sometimes serve read traffic."},{"type":"text","x":455,"y":1080,"text":"REPLICA","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":450,"y":1110,"text":"Backup copy","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"rect","x":690,"y":1050,"width":220,"height":90,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#dcfce7","explain":"The same pattern can be repeated independently for other shards."},{"type":"text","x":750,"y":1080,"text":"SHARD 2","fontSize":21,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":740,"y":1110,"text":"Primary + replica","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"text","x":75,"y":1195,"text":"BENEFITS","fontSize":24,"color":"#16a34a","explain":"Sharding mainly helps systems grow by distributing storage and workload across machines."},{"type":"text","x":90,"y":1235,"text":"✓ Horizontal scaling","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":90,"y":1270,"text":"✓ More storage capacity","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":90,"y":1305,"text":"✓ Higher parallel throughput","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":510,"y":1195,"text":"TRADE-OFFS","fontSize":24,"color":"#d97706","explain":"But sharding also adds operational complexity, especially for queries that span multiple shards."},{"type":"text","x":525,"y":1235,"text":"⚠ Cross-shard queries","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":525,"y":1270,"text":"⚠ Rebalancing data","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":525,"y":1305,"text":"⚠ Distributed transactions","fontSize":20,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"pause","duration":500},{"type":"rect","x":270,"y":1370,"width":600,"height":120,"strokeWidth":4,"color":"#1e293b","fill":"#f1f5f9","explain":"Finally, remember the core idea: partition the data, route each request to the right shard, and scale by adding machines."},{"type":"text","x":335,"y":1405,"text":"SHARDING = PARTITION + ROUTING + SCALE","fontSize":24,"color":"#1e293b"},{"type":"text","x":375,"y":1445,"text":"Data is split so one database does not carry everything","fontSize":18,"color":"#1e293b"}]}